1. d4 {Notes by Morphy} d5 2. c4 dxc4 3. e3 e5 4. Bxc4 {See
the first note on the tenth game between these players.} exd4
5. exd4 Nf6 6. Nf3 Bd6 {The correct move; in subsequent games,
however, M'Donnell almost invariably committed the error of
playing the bishop to e7. } 7. O-O O-O 8. Bg5 h6 9. Bh4 g5
{M'Donnell has been justly censured for his premature adoption
of this move in these contests. There are, undoubtedly, many
cases, in which the g-pawn may be thrown forward with
advantage; the move, however, should be made at the proper
stage of the game. } 10. Bg3 Bg4 11. Nc3 Nc6 12. Qd3 Kg7 {This
move enables White to obtain an advantage in position by
13.Ne5. 12...Bxf3 would certainly have been preferable. If, in
reply, White captures 13.Qxf3 he does not appear to possess
any marked advantage; and if 13.Qg6+ Kh8 14.Qxh6+ Nh7 15.gxf3
Nxd4 and Black's game is quite as good as his antagonist's. }
13. Ne5 Bxe5 14. dxe5 Nh5 15. Nd5 Nxg3 {We do not like this,
but it would be difficult to select a move of any greater
promise. White has the better game in consequence of Black's
erroneous twelth move.} 16. Qxg3 Bh5 17. f4 Na5 18. b3 Nxc4
19. bxc4 c6 20. Nf6 Qd4+ 21. Kh1 Bg6 22. Rad1 Qxc4 23. f5 Bh7
24. Nd7 Rfd8 25. e6 f6 26. Qc7 Rdc8 27. Qxb7 Qb5 28. Rb1 Qxb7
29. Rxb7 Kh8 30. Nxf6 Bg8 31. Rd1 Rd8 32. Rdd7 Rxd7 33. Rxd7
g4 34. Kg1 a5 35. e7 1-0